      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Substantive due process is the legal principle that the government must not only follow proper procedures but also must not enact laws that violate fundamental rights or their \"liberty\" under the Fourteenth Amendment. Historically, the Supreme Court used this power to protect the reproductive rights of women and to challenge discrimination against various unrepresented groups, though these applications often sparked significant controversy. You might frame this by showing how it transformed from a vague judicial power into a tool for civil rights activists demanding the protection of marginalized populations.",
