    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. For the lesson though, I want to make sure the students really understand why substantive due process is seen as such a vital strength of our legal system. Could you expand on the explanation to emphasize why it's considered a necessary safeguard against the \"tyranny of the majority\"? I want them to see it as a principled defense of liberty rather than just a source of legal controversy.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Substantive due process acts as a crucial antidote to the \"tyranny of the majority\" by ensuring that government actions respect fundamental liberties even when they align with popular opinion or political pressure. This principle empowers the judiciary to act as a principled defender of individual rights, preventing democratic majorities from infringing upon the inherent dignity and freedom of minority groups. By establishing that certain rights are \"inherent in human dignity\" rather than merely gifts of the state, it enforces a moral boundary on what the government is legally allowed to do. Ultimately, this framework ensures that the rule of law remains the supreme authority over the rule of the crowd, preserving a society where liberty is universally protected rather than selectively granted.",
      "source": "target",
