      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To conclude, think of substantive due process as a moral checkpoint that ensures our democracy serves the most vulnerable before it ever reaches the ballot box. While the text of the Constitution was born from fractured and specific grants of power, this principle naturally grows the moral capacity of the government to recognize rights that the authors never explicitly listed. This isn't just a legal trick; it is the essential safety net that allows a \"tyranny of the majority\" to never actually occur, making the protection of liberty a fundamental duty of our legal system.",
